Miniaturized lasers, as a kind of micro/nano-coherent light source, have been the essential elements in various photonic applications from chemical sensing to on-chip optical computing and communication.1-7 Controlled microcavity effect is a fundamental demand for micro/nanolasers in achieving different integrated photonic devices.8-13 To date, microlasers with the specific microresonators, especially the Fabry–Pérot (FP) and whispering gallery mode (WGM), have been demonstrated in inorganic semiconductors with different dimensional micro/nanostructures.14-18 Unfortunately, these traditional semiconductors usually need severe fabrication conditions including ultrahigh temperature or vacuum.19 In contract with inorganic counterparts, organic systems, with the great processability and self-assembly property,20, 21 have been also used to realize shape-controlled microlasers.22-25 Also, the organic/inorganic hybrid perovskites have demonstrated the controlled lasing behaviors.26-28 However, these organicbased photonic devices are always subjected to poor photo/chemical stability, which largely restrict their practical applications.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOF), as the crystalline material made from both metal nodes and organic ligands,29-31 could simultaneously possess excellent stability of inorganic materials and the great processable character from organic materials, emerging as a novel robust lasing system.32-34 Various MOF microcrystals with the doped laser dyes as the gain media have been utilized to demonstrate effective lasing behaviors, including the FP and WGM modes.35, 36 Recently, pure-MOF systems, as a novel gain material, have been proved to be able to solve the long-term existed problem in dye-doped MOF micro/nanolasers, such as the limited optical gain concentration and complicated materials fabrication processes.34 The formations of pure MOFs strongly rely on the coordination modes between metal nodes and organic ligands,37 which can be readily tailored by the outer environment and thus permit the modulation on the nucleation process,38-43 showing great potential for the controlled synthesis of pure MOF microcrystals with specific shape.44-47 More importantly, pure MOFs with excellent compatibility permit the introduction of diverse organic luminescent linkers,48-50 making it possible to endow MOF microcrystals with efficient optical gain. Therefore, the development of tailorable coordination modes in MOF may allow for the fabrication of MOF microcrystals with regular morphology, and help to realize cavity-tailored pure-MOF lasers.
Here, we demonstrated a strategy to control the cavity shape of MOF microlasers based on the coordination mode-tailored method. The one-dimensional (1D) microwires and 2D microplates could be successfully constructed through changing the HCl concentration to tailor the coordination modes. Both the single-crystalline microwires and microplates with efficient optical confinement functioned as low-threshold MOF microcrystal lasers. Furthermore, the distinct lasing behaviors of 1D and 2D MOF microcrystals confirm a typical shape-dependent microcavity effect: 1D microwires serve as FP microcavities, and 2D microplates lead to effective WGM microcavities. These results provide a comprehensive investigation on the well-controlled MOF growth mechanism, and open a valuable route for the exploitation of MOF-based micro/nanolasers with on-demand functions.
1,1,2,2-tetrakis(4'-(pyridin-4-yl)-[1,1'-biphenyl]-4-yl)ethene (tpbe), considering its efficient aggregation-induced emission characteristic, is selected as the organic ligand, which might induce high gain into MOFs (Figure 1A and S1).51-53 ${\mathsf{C d}}^{2+}$ ion was chosen as the metal node as it is diamagnetic and can avoid the quenching of fluorescence.54 Moreover, HCl was adopted as the reaction modulator, which could offer Cl- as the halogen ligand to tailor the coordination mode,55 offering a chance to modulate the nucleation process of MOF. In our experiments, the tpbe-based MOF microcrystals were fabricated with the classical solvothermal method (see the Supporting Information for preparation methods). In a typical synthesis, the $\mathsf{C d}(\mathsf{N O}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ and tpbe were both dissolved in the same solvent of DMF $N H_{2}O$ in a $5~\mathrm{mL}$ glass bottle, and then this reactor kept heated at $65^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for $12\mathsf{h}$ in the presence of HCl with different concentration. Finally, two kinds of X-ray-quality MOF single crystals with the compositions, $\mathsf{I C d}_{3}(\mathsf{t p b e})_{2}\mathsf{C l}_{5}]$ (FJU-96) and [Cd(tpbe)Cl2] (FJU-97), were obtained, respectively (see Supporting Information for crystal data). It is noted that the morphology features of these MOFs strongly depend on the HCl concentration: 1D microwires were prepared by choosing the high HCl concentration (Figure 1B); meanwhile 2D microdisks were synthesized under the low HCl concentration (Figure 1C).
