As shown in Figure 1D, FJU-96 contain the two types of six-coordinated nodes: Cd1, connecting to three Cl atoms and three N atoms; Cd2, connecting to four Cl atoms and two N atoms. Then, we found that the contiguous $\mathsf{C d}^{2+}$ ions can be connected by the Cl atoms and result in a Cl-doped metal chain (Figure 1F). These metal chains are further linked by tpbe molecules and result in a tridimensional framework (Figure S2). According to both the topological representation and predicted morphology of FJU-96 crystal (Figure 1H, S3 and Table S1), we can see that the tpbe molecules, induced by these metal chains, are stacking along the [001] direction, which could lead to the generation of 1D structures, making it possible to trigger the FP resonance. While, FJU-97 only possesses one type of node with Cd coordinated with two Cl atoms and four N atoms (Figure 1E). These nodes are linked by tpbe molecules to form the diamond-shaped network (Figure 1G). The spacious nature of the network allows for the entanglement between two identical networks, resulting in a doubly interpenetrated 3D framework (Figure S4). The predicted morphology and topological representation of FJU-97 crystal (Figure 1I, S5 and Table S1) show that the tpbe molecules, interlocked in the bcplane, are stacking along the [001] and [010] directions, which would benefit the growth pathway of FJU-97 microcrystal toward a 2D plate-like structure, possibly leading to the WGM-type optical resonance. These results suggested that the controlled coordination modes would lead to the distinct shapes of these tpbe-based MOF microcrystals, which is favorable for achieving different optical behaviors.
