As shown in Figure 2A and 2B, these FJU-96 microcrystals exhibit the clear 1D morphology with defect-free crystalline surfaces; meanwhile, the FJU-97 samples possess a regular square boundary. These two MOF microcrystals all exhibited the smooth surfaces and end facets (Figure S6 and S7), which would largely avoid scattering loss, and provide efficient optical feedback. Moreover, the TEM images and corresponding SAED patterns in Figure 2C and 2D reveal that the high-quality crystalline 1D wire grows along the [001] direction, while the microplate with typical 2D single-crystal feature possesses the two growing directions ([001] and [010]), which are consistent with their corresponding simulated results (Figure S2 and S4). The XRD patterns of both the FJU-96 microwires and FJU-97 microplates further verify high crystallinity of these structures (Figure S8 and S9).
More impressively, these crystalline FJU-96 microwires under UV excitation $(330-380~\mathrm{nm})$ ), exhibit stronger blue photoluminescence (PL) spots from tips and weaker light in body, indicating the typical feature of active optical waveguides (Figure 2E). In contrast, the asprepared FJU-97 microplates show brighter blue emission from all the edges and little emission in bodies (Figure 2F). This reveals that these MOF microplates are able to make photons propagate inside 2D plane, leading to strong light confinement via total internal reflection of four lateral sides. Furthermore, both the FJU-96 and FJU-97 exhibited relatively high luminescence efficiencies, which would greatly favor the optical gain and lasing action (Figure S10). As a result, these 1D and 2D MOF microcrystals were supposed to serve as both the gain media and microresonators with regular shapes.
Both the FJU-96 microwire and FJU-97 microplate with ideal optical confinement and great optical properties provide the platforms to study the lasing behavior and the microcavity effect in MOF systems (Figure S11). Here, a FJU-96 microwire was excited by a pulsed-femtosecondlaser beam ${\it400}\mathrm{nm})$ with increasing the fluence. Bright emissions could be observed at the two tips of the microwrie (Figure 3A), indicating a FP-type microcavity characteristic. Then, the spectra obtained from the tips showed multiple sharp optical modes. When the pump fluence kept increasing, the peak intensity of an optical mode $(492~\mathrm{nm})$ in the spectra was distinctly enhanced. The peak intensity in Figure 3B revealed a clear nonlinear behavior, and we calculated the lasing threshold at $\sim553~\mathrm{~nJ~}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which indicated the action of lasing in this FJU-96 microwire. When the pump fluence was higher than the threshold, the linewidth of the lasing mode at $492\ \mathrm{nm}$ instantly decreased down to ${\sim}0.52~\mathrm{nm}$ . Furthermore, simulated field intensity distribution of FJU-96 microwrie confirms that the FP-type optical modes propagate along the MOF wire (Figure 3C).
Figure 3. (A, D) PL spectra of the FJU-96 microwrie and FJU-97 microplate operating under different pump fluence. The inset is the bright-field and PL images of the FJU-96 microwrie and FJU-97 microplate. Scale bars are $10~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . (B, E) Peak intensities and FWHM with respect to the pump fluence. (C, F) Simulated field intensity distribution of the FJU-96 microwrie and FJU-97 microplate $(\lambda=492\mathrm{nm})$ ).
In comparison, the FJU-97 microplate with regular square boundary could support an effective WGM-type resonance in the 2D plane for the generation of laser emission (Figure 3F). The isolated FJU-97 microplate under excition of the same $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ -pulsed laser beam shows brighter blue emission from all edges than that from the body (Figure 3D), confirming the typical WGM-type resonance. When the pump fluence kept increasing, several optical-mode peaks were developed, and the corresponding intensity also exhibits a nonlinear behavior, which enabled us to obtain the threshold around ${\sim}442\ \mathrm{nJ}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , suggesting the occurance of WGM-type lasing from the FJU-97 sample (Figure 3E). Then, the linewidth at $492{\mathrm{nm}}$ rapidly reduces to ${\sim}0.25~\mathrm{nm}$ , revealing a better spectrum purity than the FJU-96 microwire. It is noted that the threshold in FJU-96 microwire is higher than that in FJU-97 microplate with the similar size, which can be mainly attributed to the better light-confinement of the FJU-97 microplate (Figure 3D, $Q=$ 1950) than that of the FJU-96 microwire (Figure 3A, $Q=946^{\cdot}$ ). Furthermore, both the FJU-96 and FJU-97 samples demonstrate high photostability under laser operation, which reveal the excellent stability of the pure-MOF systems (Figure S12).
The lasing spectra of 1D and 2D MOF microcrystals at different sizes were utilized to investigate the optical-cavity effect, which would help to analyze the structure–property relationship in the lasing MOF systems. Here, the lasing spectra of three distinct FJU-96 microwires in Figure 4A display the increase of the mode number with increasing the wire length (L). The change of the mode spacing $(\Delta\lambda)$ with respect to the wire length conformed to FP-type resonance, MN=N2/2Ln (n, the group refractive index). Accordingly, we derived that the nof 553 nm was around ${\sim}2.67$ (Figure 4B), which was supposed to generate the effective confinement for the FP-type modes in FJU-96 microwries. In the case of the FJU-97 microplates,the $\Delta{\lambda}$ linearly depends upon the side length $(D)$ , which satisfies the WGM equation $\Delta1=1^{2}/n2\sqrt{2}D$ Figure 4D).11 And the calculated naround 553 nm as ${\sim}2.71$ , which is very close to the reported nof MOF systems, further verifying a typical WGM lasing mechanism (Figure 4E). As a result, both 1D and 2D MOF microcrystals have relatively high group refractive index, which would well improve the confinement effect of optical resonance microcavity. The resulted $\boldsymbol{Q}$ factors of the two MOFs were both around $10^{3}$ (Figure 4C and 4F), which is pretty high for MOF-based resonators.
