In summary, we report on the shape-engineering of pure MOF microlasers through synthesizing different dimensional microcrystals (including 1D microwires and 2D microplates) based on the coordination mode-tailored method. Both the singlecrystalline microwires and microplates with efficient optical confinement served as lowthreshold MOF microcrystal lasers. More importantly, distinct lasing behaviors of 1D and 2D MOF microcrystals revealed a typical shape-dependent cavity effect: 1D microwires serve as FP microcavities; 2D microplates lead to effective WGM microcavities. These results open a new pathway for the exploitation of MOF-based micro/nanolasers with unique performances.
